{"indexedPages":16,"totalPages":16,"version":"39","text":"WISP: A WORKING-SETVIEW OFMIXTURE-OF-EXPERTSSERVING\nONEXTREMELYLOW-RESOURCEHARDWARE\nJiamu Zhang 1 Liang Wu 1 Mayank Darbari 1 Liangjie Hong 1\nABSTRACT\nModern local and agentic workloads often need large-model capacity at low concurrency, but run on GPUs that cannot keep a frontier-scale model resident. Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models are a natural fit because they activate only a small subset of experts per token, but their sparsity saves computation, not residency: the full expert pool still has to be stored, and any expert used by a layer must be in GPU memory when that layer runs. Static layer-level CPU offload makes such models fit, but transfers the expert layer in bulk on every forward pass, losing much of the sparsity advantage. We view low-resource MoE serving as aworking-setproblem on the GPU. Routed expert weights and the KV cache are two memory-demand streams competing for the same limited VRAM. We implement this view inWiSP(Working-SetPaging), a routing-aware expert pager that plugs into an unmodified serving engine and preserves byte-identical outputs. On a real 24 GiB RTX 3090, WiSP achieves up to 2.0× the decode throughput of static offload at the same memory budget when the model does not fit. A natural next step is to predict future experts and prefetch them. We find that this does not help in single-stream decode: the bottleneck is PCIe bandwidth, not prediction quality, so speculative transfers compete with demand transfers instead of hiding them. This shifts the design question from prefetching to allocation: how should one VRAM budget be divided between resident experts and the KV cache? We answer withMV-WSA (Marginal-ValueWorking-SetAllocation), which splits memory by marginal latency benefit per byte while enforcing a KV-admission floor. As a startup configurator, MV-WSA is the only policy we test that stays near-best on both prefill and decode; as a live controller, it resizes both pools while serving and reduces end-to-end time by up to 1.19× over a fixed offline split, without changing model outputs. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/nokia-applied-research/WiSP.\n1 INTRODUCTION\nMany language-model workloads now run outside large datacenters. Local coding assistants, on-device agents, and shared inference on fractional GPUs all need interactive latency at low concurrency, often on hardware that was not provisioned for frontier-scale models. These workloads still benefit from high-capacity models: an agent may switch between code, planning, tool use, and long-context reasoning within one session, so the deployment wants model capacity even when only one or a few requests are active.\nMixture-of-Experts (MoE) models (Shazeer et al.,2017;Fedus et al.,2022) are an attractive fit for this regime because they decouple capacity from per-token computation. Models such as Qwen3-30B-A3B (Qwen Team,2025), Mixtral8×7B (Jiang et al.,2024), and DeepSeek-V3 (DeepSeek-AI, 2024) place much of their parameter count in experts, but a token activates only a small routed subset. In principle,\n1Nokia, Sunnyvale, CA 94085, USA. Correspondence to: Jiamu Zhang<jiamu.zhang@nokia.com>.\nPreprint. Under Review.\nthis gives a local or agentic deployment access to a large model’s capacity without paying dense-model compute on every token.\nThe obstacle is that sparsity saves computation, but not residency. A token may use only a few experts, but the full expert pool still has to be stored somewhere, and any expert used by a layer must be in GPU memory when that layer runs. Today’s MoEs have tens to hundreds of billions of parameters, so commodity GPUs cannot keep the whole model resident even though only a small fraction is active at any step.\nA common way to run these models on small GPUs is offloading: keep most weights in host memory and transfer them over PCIe when the forward pass reaches them. This makes the model fit, and its simplest form is static layer-level offload, such as vLLM’s --cpu-offload-gb (Kwon et al.,2023). Static offload can serve a 30B MoE model in well under 10 GiB of GPU memory, but it treats an MoE layer much like a dense one: when the layer runs, it transfers the expert weights for the layer even though routing will use only a small subset for the actual computation.\narXiv:2606.21868v2 [cs.LG] 30 Aug 2026\nWISP:极低资源硬件上混合专家服务的working-set视角\nJiamu Zhang 1 Liang Wu 1 Mayank Darbari 1 Liangjie Hong 1\n摘要\n现代本地和代理式工作负载往往需要在低并发下具备大模型容量,但所运行的GPU却无法常驻一个前沿规模 的模型。混合专家(MoE)模型是自然契合的方案,因为每个token只激活一小部分专家,但其稀疏性节省 的是计算,而不是常驻内存:完整专家池仍必须被存储,而某一层用到的任何专家在该层运行时都必须位于 GPU内存中。静态的层级CPU卸载能让此类模型放得下,但会在每次前向传播时批量传输专家层,从而损失 掉大部分稀疏性优势。我们将低资源MoE服务视为GPU上的working‐set问题。路由专家权重和KV缓存是两 股相互竞争同一有限VRAM的内存需求流。我们在WiSP(Working‐Set Paging)中实现了这一视角,这是 一种路由感知的专家分页器,可接入未经修改的服务引擎,并保持字节级完全一致的输出。在真实的24 GiB RTX 3090上,当模型无法放入显存时,WiSP在相同内存预算下可达到静态卸载最高 2.0× 的解码吞吐 量。一个自然的下一步是预测未来专家并进行预取。我们发现这在单流解码中并无帮助:瓶颈是PCIe带宽, 而不是预测质量,因此推测性传输会与按需传输竞争,而不是将其隐藏。这将设计问题从预取转向分配:应 如何在常驻专家和KV缓存之间划分VRAM预算?我们用MV‐WSA(Marginal‐Value Working‐Set Allocation)来回答,它按每字节边际延迟收益来切分内存,同时强制执行KV准入下限。作为启动配置器, MV‐WSA是我们测试过的唯一在预填充和解码上都接近最优的策略;作为运行时控制器,它会在服务过程中 动态调整两个池的大小,并在不改变模型输出的情况下,相比固定的离线划分将端到端时间最多减少 1.19× 。 我们的代码公开于https://github.com/nokia‐applied‐research/WiSP。\n1 引言\n许多语言模型工作负载如今运行在大型数据中心之外。本地 编程助手、设备端智能体以及分数GPU上的共享推理,都需 要在低并发下实现交互式延迟,且往往运行在并非为前沿规 模模型所配置的硬件上。这些工作负载仍然\n受益于高容量模型:一个智能体可能在单次会话中在代码、 规划、工具使用和长上下文推理之间切换,因此即使只有一 个或少数请求处于活跃状态,部署也需要模型容量。\n专家混合(MoE)模型(Shazeer 等,2017;Fedus 等, 2022)非常适合这一场景,因为它们将容量与每 token 计 算量解耦。诸如 Qwen3‐30B‐A3B(Qwen Team,2025)、 Mixtral‐8×7B(Jiang 等,2024)和 DeepSeek‐V3( DeepSeek‐AI,2024)等模型将大部分参数量放在专家中, 但一个 token 只激活一小部分被路由的专家。原则上,\n1诺基亚,美国加利福尼亚州桑尼维尔市 94085。通讯作者: Jiamu Zhang <jiamu.zhang@nokia.com>。\n预印本。审阅中。\n这使本地或代理式部署能够获得大模型的容量,而无 需为每个 token 付出稠密模型的计算代价。\n障碍在于:稀疏性节省了计算量,但并未节省驻留内存。 一个 token 可能只使用少量专家,但完整的专家池仍必 须存储在某个地方,而且某一层用到的任何专家在该层 运行时都必须位于 GPU 内存中。如今的 MoE 拥有数百 亿到数千亿参数,因此即便在任意步骤中只有一小部分 处于活跃状态,消费级 GPU 也无法将整个模型常驻内存。\n在小显存 GPU 上运行这些模型的一种常见方式是卸载:将 大部分权重保留在主机内存中,并在前向传播到达它们时通 过 PCIe 传输。这使得模型能够放入显存,其最简单的形式 是静态层级卸载,例如 vLLM 的 ‐‐cpu‐offload‐gb( Kwon 等,2023)。静态卸载可以在远低于 10GiB 的 GPU 显存中服务一个 30B 的 MoE 模型,但它对待 MoE 层的方式与稠密层几乎相同:当该层运行时,它会传输该层 的专家权重,尽管路由在实际计算中只会使用其中的一小部 分。\narXiv:2606.21868v2 [cs.LG] 30 Aug 2026\n\n\n Working-Set Paging for Low-Resource MoE Serving\nSeveral specialized systems improve on this baseline, but they make different tradeoffs. Some keep part of the MoE computation on the CPU, as in ktransformers, which can be fast but relies on Intel AMX (KVCache.AI,2024), while others target edge deployment by changing the weight format or precision, as in llama.cpp (Gerganov et al.,2023). A third line pipelines, prefetches, or predicts expert transfers to hide communication under computation (Cao et al.,2025; Du et al.,2024;Eliseev & Mazur,2023). This last line is closest in spirit to WiSP, but its main lever is overlap: it works best when there is enough computation or batching to hide data movement. Our setting is the other end of the spectrum: in low-concurrency interactive serving, each step activates only a small routed subset of experts, but decode exposes little compute behind which to hide PCIe transfers.\nThe missing piece is therefore not another way to stream the next experts, but a way to decide which experts should remain resident, and how that resident set should share memory with the KV cache.\nServing is a working-set problem.In low-resource MoE serving, the scarce resources are GPU memory and PCIe bandwidth, not arithmetic. This makes the problem look much like the classicalworking-setproblem (Denning, 1968;1970). GPU memory is the small fast store, while host memory is the backing store, and the model generates two streams of memory demand: routed expert weights, determined by the MoE router, and KV-cache blocks, determined by the active tokens. Both have to be resident when used, and both draw from the same VRAM budget.\nUnder this view, good serving is memory management. The system should keep the experts a workload reuses in GPU memory, leave enough space for the KV blocks of active requests, and avoid admitting more concurrent work than those two working sets can support. This turns expert offload and KV-cache management from two separate knobs into one allocation problem: every byte given to resident experts is a byte taken away from KV, and vice versa. The same abstraction applies to any serving stack with routed experts and an autoregressive KV cache, including MoE LLMs and emerging MoE vision–language models.\nThe same view also shapes the system design. WiSP changes where weights live and how memory is split, not the computation itself. It leaves the serving engine, kernels, model weights, and numerical precision unchanged, so its outputs are byte-identical to the underlying engine.\nWiSP implements this policy as a drop-in plug-in. It reuses the engine’s existing expert-map indirection, which maps logical expert ids to physical slots, and makes those slots a small GPU scratch space containing only the resident experts. As the working set changes, WiSP updates the map rather than changing the kernel, model, or precision. This\nkeeps the mechanism architecture-independent for any MoE model the engine can already serve. Section3describes the mechanism in detail.\nWhat we find.At matched GPU-memory budgets in the sub-fitting regime, WiSP’s routing-aware paging improves decode throughput by up to 2.0× over static offload, measured on a physically constrained 24 GiB RTX 3090 (PCIe 4.0). This is the regime WiSP is designed for: when the model cannot be kept resident, caching only the experts a workload reuses moves far fewer bytes than static layer-level offload. Once enough VRAM is available to keep the model resident, static residency is faster because WiSP’s paging indirection becomes unnecessary; we report that crossover explicitly.\nWe then test the natural next idea: use routing predictions to prefetch experts and hide page faults. In low-concurrency decode, this provides little benefit and can hurt, because there is too little computation behind which to hide a PCIe transfer, and thus decode is bandwidth-bound rather than prediction-bound; speculative transfers mostly compete with demand transfers for the same link. Routing information is therefore more useful as a memory signal—which experts are worth keeping resident—than as a latency signal for prefetching.\nThis shifts the paper’s main question from prefetching to allocation. Expert weights and the KV cache share one VRAM budget: every byte given to resident experts is a byte taken away from KV, and vice versa. We introduce MV-WSA, a marginal-value allocator that splits this budget by equalizing latency benefit per byte, while enforcing a KV-admission floor so the scheduler can still admit the requested concurrency. MV-WSA can be used as a startup configurator, which fixes the split before serving, or as an online controller, which resizes the expert and KV pools while requests are running. In our real-serving allocation test, the configurator is the only policy near-best on both prefill and decode; the online controller further improves end-to-end performance by up to 1.19× over the best offline split at the same VRAM budget, without changing model outputs.\nWe make four contributions:\n• A working-set formulation of low-resource MoE serving.We frame routed expert weights and the KV cache as two memory-demand streams competing for the same limited VRAM. This gives a common vocabulary for expert residency, KV allocation, thrashing, and admission, and separates our low-concurrency setting from high-batch datacenter serving (Section2).\n• A drop-in routing-aware expert pager.We implement expert paging through the serving engine’s ex\n面向低资源MoE服务的工作集分页\n若干专用系统在这一基线上有所改进,但它们做出了不同的 权衡。有些将部分 MoE 计算保留在 CPU 上,例如 ktransformers,它可以很快,但依赖 Intel AMX( KVCache.AI,2024);而另一些则通过改变权重格式或精 度来面向边缘部署,例如 llama.cpp(Gerganov 等, 2023)。第三条路线则通过流水线、预取或预测专家传输 来将通信隐藏在计算之下(Cao 等,2025;Du 等, 2024;Eliseev & Mazur,2023)。最后这条路线在精神 上与 WiSP 最接近,但其主要手段是重叠:只有当有足够的 计算或批处理来隐藏数据移动时,它才能发挥最佳效果。我 们的场景处于光谱的另一端:在低并发的交互式服务中,每 一步只激活一小部分被路由的专家,但解码过程暴露出的计 算量很少,无法用来隐藏 PCIe 传输。\n因此,缺失的一环不是另一种流式加载下一批专家权 重的方法,而是一种决定哪些专家应常驻显存、以及 该常驻集合如何与KV缓存共享内存的机制。\n服务本质上是一个工作集问题。在低资源MoE服务中, 稀缺资源是GPU显存和PCIe带宽,而非算力。这使得 该问题与经典的工作集问题(Denning, 1968; 1970)极为相似。GPU显存是小型快速存储,而主机 内存是后备存储,模型产生两股内存需求流:由 MoE路由器决定的路由专家权重,以及由活跃token 决定的KV缓存块。两者在使用时都必须常驻,且都从 同一VRAM预算中分配。\n在此视角下,良好的服务就是内存管理。系统应将工 作负载重复使用的专家保留在GPU显存中,为活跃请 求的KV块留出足够空间,并避免接纳超出这两个工作 集支撑能力的并发任务。这将专家卸载和KV缓存管理 从两个独立的调节旋钮转变为一个统一的分配问题: 分配给常驻专家的每一个字节,都是从KV缓存中夺走 的字节,反之亦然。这一抽象适用于任何具有路由专 家和自回归KV缓存的服务栈,包括MoE大语言模型和 新兴的MoE视觉‐语言模型。\n相同的视角也塑造了系统设计。WiSP 改变的是权重 的存放位置以及内存的划分方式,而非计算本身。它 保持服务引擎、内核、模型权重和数值精度不变,因 此其输出与底层引擎逐字节一致。\nWiSP 将该策略实现为即插即用的插件。它复用引擎 已有的专家映射间接层,该层将逻辑专家 ID 映射到 物理槽位,并将这些槽位变为仅包含常驻专家的小型 GPU 暂存空间。随着工作集的变化,WiSP 更新映射, 而不是更改内核、模型或精度。这\n使得该机制与架构无关,适用于引擎已能服务的任何 MoE 模型。第 3 节详细描述了该机制。\n我们的发现。在模型无法完全驻留的情况下,于匹配 的 GPU 内存预算下,WiSP 的路由感知分页相比静态 卸载可将解码吞吐量提升最高达 2.0× ,测量基于物理 受限的 24 GiB RTX 3090(PCIe 4.0)。这正是 WiSP 所针对的场景:当模型无法保持常驻时,仅缓 存工作负载重复使用的专家所移动的字节数远少于静 态层级卸载。一旦有足够的显存使模型常驻,静态常 驻会更快,因为 WiSP 的分页间接寻址变得不必要; 我们明确报告了这一交叉点。\n我们随后测试了自然的下一步思路:利用路由预测来 预取专家并隐藏页面错误。在低并发解码中,这带来 的收益甚微,甚至可能有害,因为可供隐藏 PCIe 传 输的计算量太少,因此解码受带宽限制而非受预测限 制;推测性传输大多与需求传输竞争同一链路。因此, 路由信息作为内存信号——哪些专家值得保持常驻—— 比作为预取的延迟信号更有用。\n这将论文的核心问题从预取转向了分配。专家权重和 KV缓存共享同一VRAM预算:分配给常驻专家的每一 个字节都是从KV中拿走的,反之亦然。我们提出了 MV‐WSA,一种边际价值分配器,通过均衡每字节的 延迟收益来划分这一预算,同时强制执行KV准入下限, 使调度器仍能接纳所请求的并发量。MV‐WSA可作为 启动配置器使用,在服务开始前确定划分比例;也可 作为在线控制器使用,在请求运行期间调整专家池和 KV池的大小。在我们的真实服务分配测试中,配置器 是唯一在预填充和解码两方面都接近最优的策略;在 线控制器在相同VRAM预算下,进一步将端到端性能 较最佳离线划分提升最多 1.19× ,且不改变模型输出。\n我们做出四项贡献:\n• 低资源MoE服务的工作集公式化。我们将路由专 家权重和KV缓存视为争夺同一有限VRAM的两条内 存需求流。这为专家驻留、KV分配、抖动和准入提 供了共同的术语体系,并将我们的低并发场景与高 批次数据中心服务区分开来(第2节)。\n• 一个即插即用的路由感知专家分页器。我们通过服 务引擎现有的专家映射间接层来实现专家分页,\n\n\n Working-Set Paging for Low-Resource MoE Serving\nisting expert-map indirection, without changing kernels, model weights, or quantization. On a physically constrained 24 GiB RTX 3090, WiSP improves decode throughput by up to 2.0× over static offload at matched VRAM when the model does not fit (Sections3,4).\n• A negative result on prefetching in low-concurrency decode.We show that routing-based expert prefetching is not the main speed lever in this regime: single-stream decode is PCIe-bandwidth-bound, not predictionbound, so speculative transfers compete with demand transfers instead of hiding them. The routing signal is more useful for sizing the resident expert set than for reducing latency through prefetching (Section4).\n• MV-WSA for joint expert–KV allocation.We introduce a marginal-value allocator that divides VRAM between resident experts and the KV cache under an admission floor. As a startup configurator, it is near-best on both prefill and decode in our real-serving allocation test. As an online controller, it physically resizes both pools during serving and improves end-to-end performance by up to 1.19× over the best offline split at the same VRAM budget, byte-identically (Sections3,5).\n2 BACKGROUND: SERVING AS A\nWORKING-SETPROBLEM\n2.1 The Working-Set View\nA program’sworking setis the set of memory pages it has used recently (Denning,1968;1970). If that set stays resident, execution is fast, but if the resident set is too small, the program repeatedly faults on pages it just used and begins tothrash. If the system admits more concurrent work than the combined working sets can support, the whole machine thrashes. Low-resource MoE serving has the same shape: each active token needs a routed subset of experts in every MoE layer, and each active request needs its KV blocks. Both must be resident in GPU memory when used. As concurrency, context length, or the routed expert union grows, the two working sets compete for the same limited VRAM.\nThis view is useful because expert use is sparse, but it is not structureless. A token activates only k of N experts, so keeping every expert resident wastes memory when VRAM is limited. At the same time, a short sequence of decode steps touches much more than a tiny per-token set, so the cache cannot shrink to just the next token’s experts. The opportunity is reuse at the workload scale: across bursts and sessions, a workload tends to revisit a stable subset of experts. Caching that routed working set, rather than the whole expert pool, is what makes expert paging viable on a device that cannot keep the full model resident.\nThe KV cache is the other half of this memory problem. PagedAttention (Kwon et al.,2023) already manages KV as blocks that can be allocated, reused, and spilled. Expert weights, however, are usually handled by a separate mechanism: either kept fully resident or streamed in bulk from the host. The two mechanisms still draw from the same VRAM budget. Every byte used for resident experts is a byte unavailable to KV, and vice versa. Seen this way, low-resource MoE serving is not two independent problems—expert offload and KV management—but one allocation problem over two competing working sets.\n2.2 Why the Serving Regime Matters\nThe best strategy depends on concurrency. In high-batch datacenter serving, a layer may activate many or most experts across the batch. The per-layer working set is then close to the whole expert bank, and there is enough computation to overlap expert transfers with execution. Streaming and prefetching are natural in that setting (Liu et al.,2026;Cao et al.,2025).\nWiSP targets the other end of this spectrum: one or a few interactive streams on a GPU that cannot hold the full model. Here each step activates only a small routed subset of experts, and decode exposes little computation behind which to hide PCIe transfers. The useful question is therefore not how to hide the next transfer, but which experts should remain resident across steps and how much VRAM should be left for KV. Section4measures this directly.\n2.3 Scope and Generality\nThe expert-paging mechanism applies to any model with routed experts that the serving stack presents as an expert bank. The full working-set allocation problem applies when the model also has an autoregressive KV cache, because resident experts and KV blocks compete for the same memory budget. Models without a KV cache, such as diffusion MoEs or pure recurrent SSM–MoE variants, can still use expert paging to reduce resident memory. They do not exercise the full expert–KV allocation problem, so we treat them as mechanism-generalization cases rather than headline throughput targets. Table1summarizes this distinction.\nWhere WiSP sits among existing systems.Most prior\nMoE-offloading work targets autoregressive MoE LLMs. The main axis separating these systems is the serving regime. High-batch systems can stream or prefetch experts because they have enough computation to overlap transfers. WiSP targets low-concurrency serving, where overlap is limited and reuse-based caching matters more. It changes residency and allocation policy, not the model computation, so the engine’s outputs remain byte-identical.\nThe closest concurrent system is FluxMoE (Liu et al.,2026).\n面向低资源MoE服务的工作集分页\n无需更改内核、模型权重或量化。在物理受限的 24GiB RTX 3090 上,当模型无法完全容纳时, WiSP 在匹配显存条件下将解码吞吐量较静态卸 载提升至多 2.0× (第 3、4 节)。\n• 在低并发解码中预取的一个负面结果。我们表明, 基于路由的专家预取在此场景下并不是主要的速度 杠杆:单流解码受限于PCIe带宽,而非受限于预测, 因此推测性传输会与需求传输竞争,而不是将其隐 藏。路由信号在确定常驻专家集大小方面比通过预 取降低延迟更有用(第4节)。\n• 用于专家–KV联合分配的MV‐WSA。我们引入了一种边 际价值分配器,在准入下限约束下将VRAM分配给常驻专 家和KV缓存。作为启动配置器,在我们的真实服务分配 测试中,它在预填充和解码两方面都接近最优。作为在 线控制器,它在服务期间物理调整两个池的大小,在相 同VRAM预算下,相比最佳离线划分,端到端性能提升最 高可达 1.19× ,且字节完全相同(第3、5节)。\n2 背景:作为工作集问题的服务\n2.1 工作集视角\n程序的工作集是它最近使用过的内存页集合( Denning, 1968; 1970)。如果该集合保持驻留,执行 就很快;但如果驻留集太小,程序就会反复对其刚刚使 用过的页面发生缺页,并开始抖动。如果系统接纳的并 发工作超过合并工作集所能支持的量,整个机器就会抖 动。低资源 MoE 服务具有相同的形态:每个活跃 token 都需要每个 MoE 层中被路由到的专家子集,而每 个活跃请求都需要其 KV 块。两者在使用时都必须驻留 在 GPU 内存中。随着并发量、上下文长度或路由专家并 集的增长,这两个工作集会争夺同一有限的 VRAM。\n这一视角之所以有用,是因为专家的使用虽然是稀疏的, 但并非无结构。一个 token 只激活 N 个专家中的 k 个,因此在显存有限时,让每个专家都常驻会浪费内存。 与此同时,一段短的解码步骤序列所触及的专家远不止 每 token 的极小集合,因此缓存不能缩小到仅保留下一 个 token 的专家。机会在于工作负载尺度上的复用:跨 突发和会话,工作负载往往会重新访问一个稳定的专家 子集。缓存这一路由工作集,而非整个专家池,正是使 专家分页在无法将完整模型常驻的设备上可行的关键。\nKV 缓存是这一内存问题的另一半。PagedAttention (Kwon 等,2023)已经将 KV 作为可分配、可复用、 可溢出的块来管理。然而,专家权重通常由另一套机 制处理:要么完全常驻,要么从主机批量流式加载。 这两种机制仍然从同一显存预算中取用。用于常驻专 家的每一个字节,都是 KV 无法使用的字节,反之亦 然。由此看来,低资源 MoE 服务并非两个独立问题—— 专家卸载与 KV 管理——而是两个相互竞争的工作集 之上的同一个分配问题。\n2.2 为什么服务机制很重要\n最佳策略取决于并发量。在高批量数据中心服务中,一 个层可能会在整个批次中激活许多或大多数专家。此时 每层工作集接近于整个专家库,并且有足够的计算来将 专家传输与执行重叠起来。流式传输和预取在这种场景 下是自然的选择(Liu et al., 2026; Cao et al., 2025)。\nWiSP 针对的是这一频谱的另一端:在无法容纳完整模型 的 GPU 上运行一个或少数几个交互式流。此时每一步仅 激活一小部分被路由的专家,而解码阶段几乎没有计算 量可用于掩盖 PCIe 传输。因此,有用的问题不在于如 何隐藏下一次传输,而在于哪些专家应在各步骤间保持 驻留,以及应为 KV 留出多少显存。第 4 节直接对此进 行了测量。\n2.3 范围与通用性\n专家分页机制适用于任何具有路由专家且服务栈将其 呈现为专家库的模型。完整工作集分配问题在模型同 时具有自回归 KV 缓存时出现,因为驻留专家和 KV 块竞争同一内存预算。没有 KV 缓存的模型,例如扩 散 MoE 或纯循环 SSM–MoE 变体,仍可使用专家分 页来减少驻留内存。它们不涉及完整的专家–KV 分配 问题,因此我们将其视为机制泛化案例,而非主要吞 吐量目标。表 1 总结了这一区别。\nWiSP 在现有系统中所处的位置。以往大多数 MoE 卸 载工作都针对自回归 MoE 大语言模型。区分这些系 统的主要维度是服务模式。高批量系统能够流式传输 或预取专家,因为它们有足够的计算来重叠传输。 WiSP 面向低并发服务,在这种情况下重叠有限,基 于复用的缓存更为重要。它改变的是驻留和分配策略, 而非模型计算,因此引擎的输出保持逐字节完全相同。\n最接近的并发系统是 FluxMoE(Liu 等,2026)。\n\n\n Working-Set Paging for Low-Resource MoE Serving\nTable 1. Where the working-set view applies.Expert stream= routed experts that can be paged;KV stream= an autoregressive key–value cache that co-contends for memory. WiSP’s mechanism needs the former; its full theory needs both.\nArchitecture MoE variant Representative Expert stream KV stream WiSP coverage\nDense LLM×(dense) Llama-3, Qwen2.5×✓n/a SSM (pure)✓BlackMamba (Anthony et al.,2024)✓×(recurrent state) mechanism only MoE LLM✓Mixtral, Qwen3-MoE, DeepSeek-V3✓ ✓full (primary) MoE VLM✓DeepSeek-VL2, Kimi-VL, Aria✓ ✓full (breadth) Hybrid SSM–MoE✓Jamba (Lieber et al.,2024)✓ ✓(attention)full MoE diffusion LLM✓LLaDA-MoE (Zhu et al.,2025)✓×(bidirectional) mechanism only MoE image diffusion✓DiT-MoE (Fei et al.,2024)✓×(latent denoise) mechanism only\nLike WiSP, it trades expert residency against KV capacity, but it uses a stream-and-evict policy tuned for high-batch overlap. WiSP instead uses cache-and-reuse for the regime where there is little compute to hide transfer latency. Beyond autoregressive LLMs, MoE VLMs, hybrid models, and diffusion MoEs use different serving stacks; WiSP attaches to them either as the same serving stack plug-in or through a small wrapper. AppendixDgives the full design-space and serving-stack comparisons (Tables5,6).\n3 WISP\nWiSP separates low-resource MoE serving into two decisions. First, arouting-aware expert pager(§3.2) decides which experts are resident in GPU memory. It uses the serving engine’s existing expert map indirection to map routed expert ids onto a small GPU scratch space, so the kernel, weights, precision, and outputs are unchanged. Second, MV-WSA(Marginal-Value Working-Set Allocation,§3.3– §3.5) decides how much of the available VRAM should go to that expert scratch versus the KV cache. The pager manages residencywithinthe expert pool; MV-WSA moves the boundarybetweenthe expert and KV pools. We use MV-WSA in two modes: offline, as a startup configurator that fixes the split before serving, and online, as a controller that resizes both pools while requests are running. Figure1 summarizes the design.\n3.1 Setting and Notation\nThe model hasLMoE layers withNexperts each, and the router selects k≪N experts per token per layer. At step t, layer l selects R(t)\nl ⊆ {1, . . . , N} , and every expert e∈\nR(t)\nl must be resident when that layer executes. After fixed GPU costs are reserved—non-expert weights, activations, CUDA context, and other runtime state—the device has a pageable budget B shared by resident experts and the KV cache:\nB=B exp +B kv, f≜B exp/B∈(0,1).(1)\nThe split f is MV-WSA’s control variable. If one expert occupies Me bytes, then increasing the per-layer resident cap by one expert costs a≜LM e bytes across all MoE\nlayers, so the expert budget supports C=B exp/a resident experts per layer. The KV budget supports κ=B kv/b KV blocks, where b is the GPU memory per KV block summed over all layers. At a fixed budget B, WiSP’s allocation problem is to choose where this split should land.\n3.2 The Expert Pager\nThe allocator can only trade expert memory against KV memory if the expert side is itself pageable. WiSP provides this substrate with the engine’s existing expert-map indirection, without changing the fused-MoE kernel, model weights, or precision.\nExpert-map indirection.Modern fused-MoE kernels accept an expert mapπ that maps logical expert ids to physical slots. WiSP makes those physical slots a small GPU scratch space with capacity C per layer. The full expert weights remain in host memory; at each layer, WiSP ensures that the routed experts are present in scratch, updates π, and dispatches the unmodified kernel. A page fault occurs when a routed expert is not currently resident, in which case WiSP copies it from the host master and evicts an older resident expert using LRU. All dynamic behavior is in the map and the scratch contents, not in the computation.\nPer-step protocol.Algorithm1shows the per-layer decode step. Given the routed expert set R, WiSP copies only the missing experts, assigns them scratch slots, refreshes the LRU state for the experts used by this step, and calls the stock fused-MoE kernel with the updated expert map. The key invariant is exactness. When the kernel runs, every routed expert id maps to a scratch slot containing the same bytes the full expert tensor would have supplied. The kernel is unchanged, so WiSP’s output matches the unpaged engine up to the engine’s own floating-point reproducibility. For large prefill batches whose expert union exceeds C, WiSP groups tokens so that each group fits the scratch and reassembles the outputs in token order; this path is an implementation detail and does not trigger in our single-stream decode measurements.\n面向低资源MoE服务的Working-Set分页 ving\n表1. 工作集视角的适用范围。专家流 = 可被分页的路由专家;KV流 = 与专家流争用内存的自回归键值缓存。WiSP的机制需要前者; 其完整理论需要两者。\n架构 MoE变体 代表 专家流 KV 流 WiSP 覆盖范围 稠密 LLM × (稠密) Llama‐3、Qwen2.5 × ✓ n/a