pert residency, KV capacity, and admission together. On the systems side, PagedAttention in vLLM (Kwon et al.,2023) and RadixAttention in SGLang (Zheng et al.,2024) brought a paging and prefix-sharing discipline to the KV reference stream; we bring the same discipline to the expert stream and treat the two as co-contending working sets over one store.\nA line of batch-oriented systems serves MoEs on memoryconstrained GPUs by overlapping transfer with compute: MoE-Lightning (Cao et al.,2025) pipelines paged weights and reports up to 10.3× on a T4, SiDA-MoE (Du et al.,2024) uses an offline hash predictor of active experts (approximate, ∼1% accuracy drop), and Mixtraloffloading (Eliseev & Mazur,2023) streams experts for a single stream. These target high throughput at large batch, where there is ample compute to hide transfers. A parallel line predicts or caches experts to cut miss latency rather than stream blindly: MoE-Infinity (Xue et al.,2024) keeps a sparsity-aware expert cache driven by activation tracking on personal machines; Pre-gated MoE (Hwang et al.,2024) co-designs the model to pre-compute the next layer’s experts one step ahead; HybriMoE (Zhong et al.,2025) splits expert work across CPU and GPU with an impact-driven prefetch; FineMoE (Yu et al.,2026) retrieves per-request expert maps by semantic and trajectory similarity to guide prefetching; and DyMoE (Huang et al.,2026) assigns experts mixed precision (down to skipping) to shrink edge transfers. Most share WiSP’s premise that expert residency is the bottleneck, but spend the routing signal on prefetching or overlap for speed—which Section4shows is a poor fit for single-stream decode, where no compute hides a transfer. WiSP instead spends the signal on sizing and treats experts and KV as one budget. The closest concurrent work is FluxMoE (Liu et al.,2026), which, like WiSP, builds on vLLM and reclaims expert VRAM for the KV cache, but adopts a stream-and-evict policy—paging an expert in just before its layer and evicting it immediately, pipelined with layer compute—to maximize high-batch throughput. WiSP differs in four ways: it targets the low-concurrency, cacheand-reuse operating point, where Section4shows there is no compute to overlap; it ships as a pure plug-in over the unmodified engine with no virtual-memory machinery; rather than a fixed, overlap-tuned residency plan itallocatesthe expert↔KV split by marginal value (MV-WSA), online and subject to an admission floor, and physically resizes both pools while serving; and it contributes the working-set framing, the measurement of when prediction does and does not pay off, and the cross-architecture breadth. The systems are\n面向低资源MoE服务的工作集分页\nZhu, F., You, Z., Xing, Y., Huang, Z., et al. LLaDA‐MoE: 一种稀疏MoE扩散语言模型。arXiv预印本arXiv:2509.24389, 2025年。\n相关工作\nWiSP的概念基础是Denning的工作集模型与抖动理论 (Denning, 1968; 1970);我们的贡献在于将低资 源MoE服务识别为该模型的一个直接实例,并运用这 套术语来统一推理专家驻留、KV容量与准入问题。在 系统层面,vLLM中的PagedAttention(Kwon et al., 2023)和SGLang中的RadixAttention(Zheng et al., 2024)为KV引用流引入了分页与前缀共享的 纪律;我们将同样的纪律引入专家流,并将二者视为 在同一存储上共同竞争的工作集。\n一系列面向批处理的系统通过在内存受限的GPU上重叠 传输与计算来服务MoE:MoE‐Lightning(Cao等, 2025)对分页权重进行流水线处理,并报告在T4上可达 10.3× ,SiDA‐MoE(Du等,2024)使用活跃专家的离 线哈希预测器(近似,精度下降 ∼1%),而 Mixtral‐offloading(Eliseev & Mazur,2023)为单一 流流式传输专家。这些系统面向大批量下的高吞吐量, 此时有充足的计算来隐藏传输。另一条平行研究路线通 过预测或缓存专家来减少未命中延迟,而非盲目流式传 输:MoE‐Infinity(Xue等,2024)在个人机器上通过激 活追踪维护稀疏感知的专家缓存;Pre‐gated MoE( Hwang等,2024)协同设计模型以提前一步预计算下一 层的专家;HybriMoE(Zhong等,2025)通过影响驱动 的预取将专家工作分配到CPU和GPU上;FineMoE( Yu等,2026)通过语义和轨迹相似性检索每请求的专家 映射以指导预取;DyMoE(Huang等,2026)为专家分 配混合精度(直至跳过)以缩小边缘传输。大多数与 WiSP共享专家驻留是瓶颈这一前提,但将路由信号用于 预取或重叠以提升速度——第4节表明,这对于单流解码 并不适合,因为没有计算可以隐藏传输。WiSP反而将信 号用于容量规划,并将专家和KV视为统一预算。最接近 的同期工作是FluxMoE(Liu等,2026),它像WiSP一 样构建于vLLM之上,并回收专家VRAM用于KV缓存,但 采用流式驱逐策略——在其层之前调入专家并立即驱逐, 与层计算流水线化——以最大化大批量吞吐量。WiSP有 四点不同:它面向低并发、缓存复用的工作点,第4节表 明此时没有计算可重叠;它以纯插件形式发布在未修改 的引擎之上,无需虚拟内存机制;它不采用固定的、针 对重叠调优的驻留方案,而是通过边际价值(MVWSA)在线分配专家↔KV的划分,并受准入下限约束, 同时在服务过程中物理调整两个池的大小;它还贡献了 工作集框架、对预测何时有效何时无效的测量,以及跨 架构的广度。这些系统是\n\n\n Working-Set Paging for Low-Resource MoE Serving\ncomplementary across the concurrency axis.\nConcurrent with this work, FreeToken (Yang et al.,2026) co-designs a full edge-native MoE serving stack—pipelined full-layer expert loading for prefill, semantic-aware state caching for agentic context edits, and an elastic expert/KV memory mechanism—and, at decode, serves residual expert misses through a second channel: executing missing experts in place on the CPU, splitting each layer’s misses between PCIe fill and CPU compute in proportion to profiled bandwidths. This adds a degree of freedom that allocation alone cannot supply under the single-stream PCIe ceiling of Section4: where WiSP can only fetch a missing expert, FreeToken may also compute it where it resides. The two designs are complementary along the allocation axis: FreeToken provides a runtime mechanism for reconfiguring the expert/KV split but does not specify a policy for choosing or adapting that split, which is the question MV-WSA answers, and extending MV-WSA’s marginal-value rule to price a second service channel (fetch versus compute) is a natural next step.\nPrior work on MoE routing prediction and speculative loading motivates the predictor we build in Section4; we evaluate it directly and report a negative speed result for the low-concurrency regime, which clarifies that the signal is better spent on memory than latency. KV-cache compression methods such as KIVI (Liu et al.,2024b) reduce the other side of the joint working set and compose with WiSP rather than competing with it. Finally, WiSP is evaluated across MoE LLMs (Shazeer et al.,2017;Fedus et al.,2022; Jiang et al.,2024;DeepSeek-AI,2024;Qwen Team,2025), MoE VLMs (Wu et al.,2024;Kimi Team,2025;Li et al., 2024), a hybrid SSM-MoE (Lieber et al.,2024;Anthony et al.,2024), and MoE diffusion models for images (Peebles & Xie,2023;Fei et al.,2024) and language (Nie et al.,2025; Zhu et al.,2025); we use these only as test subjects and change none of them.\nB DYNAMICDUAL-RESIZECONTROLLAW\nRecall a=LM e. At a drained barrier the conserved budget is measured directly, B=κb+Ca , and the target sizes C ̃, ̃κare\n ̃κ= max κmin,⌈(1 +h)p⌉ ,(6)\nC ̃= clip ⌊(B− ̃κ b)/a⌋, Cmin, Cmax ,(7)\n ̃κ← ⌊(B− C ̃ a)/b⌋,(8)\nwhere p is the burst’s peak KV occupancy (blocks actually used) and h the KV headroom. Equation (6) promotes the admission floor (4) from a one-shot startup constraint to a continuously enforced invariant; (7) is the equimarginal “remainder to experts” rule for a KV step curve; and the recompute (8) reclaims any slack the cap clamp leaves, so\nthe move never overcommits (C ̃ a+  ̃κ b≤B).\nTheexpert-scratch resizegrows or shrinks the per-layer GPU scratch S to C ̃ slots, re-paging now-resident experts from W cpu and dropping victims on a shrink, with π, π−1 and the LRU ticks resized alongside. TheKV-pool resize reallocates the worker’s per-layer KV tensors to  ̃κblocks and rebuilds the scheduler’s shared block pool in place, preserving the cached null block identity, done only when the pool is fully drained. To keep the transient footprint at max(old,new)≤B rather than their sum, the controller always frees the shrinking side before growing the other (on an expert grow: shrink KV, then grow scratch; on a shrink: the reverse). Every move is wrapped so a failed resize is skipped—the engine is left in its prior, usable state—and a cap dead-zone ∆ suppresses moves smaller than ∆ experts so the split is stable under round-to-round noise.\nC IMPLEMENTATIONDETAILS\nWiSP registers through the standard vllm.general plugins entry point, so a single pip install suffices; the plug-in fires in every vLLM process before any model class is imported and patches three methods on the fused-MoE module—weight creation, post-load processing, and the forward. Weight creation allocates the expert masters on pinned host memory (this is what avoids the load-time GPU spike); post-load processing builds the per-layer C-slot scratch and the maps π, π−1; the forward runs Algorithm1and dispatches the unmodified kernel with π as expert map. The FP8 block-quantized path pages four tensors per expert (the weights and their block scales) and forces the Triton backend to bypass DeepGEMM’s load-time post-processing, which assumes full residency. The dynamic controller’s two primitives are resize layer caps (grow or shrink the expert scratch, re-paging from the host master) and a KV-pool reallocation that rebuilds the scheduler’s block pool in place (AppendixB).\nD SERVINGDESIGNSPACE\nThis appendix expands the placement of WiSP among existing systems summarized in Section2.3. Table5compares WiSP against prior autoregressive MoE-LLM serving systems along the axes that matter for our setting (routingawareness, byte-identity, delivery, and target regime), and Table6lists the native serving stacks of the non-AR MoE modalities and how WiSP attaches to each.\nE THECO-ACTIVATIONPREDICTOR\nSection4uses a lightweight routing predictor to test whether expert prediction can hide page faults in low-concurrency\n面向低资源 MoE 服务的工作集分页\n在并发轴上互补的。\n与本工作同期,FreeToken(Yang 等,2026)共同设 计了一套完整的边缘原生 MoE 服务栈——用于预填充的 流水线式全层专家加载、面向智能体上下文编辑的语义 感知状态缓存,以及弹性专家/KV 内存机制——并且在 解码阶段,通过第二条通道来处理剩余的专家未命中: 在 CPU 上就地执行缺失的专家,按照经性能剖析得到的 带宽比例,将每一层的未命中在 PCIe 填充与 CPU 计算 之间进行拆分。这增加了一个仅靠分配无法提供的自由 度,而这是在 Section 4 的单流 PCIe 上限下无法实现 的:WiSP 只能获取缺失的专家,而 FreeToken 还可以 在专家所在处直接计算它。这两种设计在分配轴上互补: FreeToken 提供了一种用于重新配置专家/KV 划分的运 行时机制,但并未给出选择或自适应调整该划分的策略, 而这正是 MV‐WSA 所回答的问题;将 MV‐WSA 的边际 价值规则扩展到为第二条服务通道(获取与计算)定价, 则是自然的下一步。\n先前关于MoE路由预测和推测性加载的工作启发了我们在第 4节中构建的预测器;我们对其进行了直接评估,并报告了 在低并发场景下的负面加速结果,这阐明了该信号更适合用 于内存而非延迟。KV缓存压缩方法(如KIVI(Liu等, 2024b))减少了联合工作集的另一侧,并与WiSP组合使 用而非相互竞争。最后,WiSP在MoE大语言模型( Shazeer等,2017;Fedus等,2022;Jiang等,2024; DeepSeek‐AI,2024;Qwen Team,2025)、MoE视觉 语言模型(Wu等,2024;Kimi Team,2025;Li等, 2024)、混合SSM‐MoE(Lieber等,2024;Anthony等, 2024)以及用于图像(Peebles & Xie,2023;Fei等, 2024)和语言(Nie等,2025;Zhu等,2025)的MoE扩 散模型上进行了评估;我们仅将这些作为测试对象,未对其 做任何修改。\nB 动态双重调整控制律\n回顾 a = LMe。在已排空的屏障处,守恒预算被直接\n